{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792510628,"nonce":"1QPDBz2tHjlcielXCI5OKfYJRbw=","message":"Implemented in 5384cfec269591b2a63f5f5ed16a3fdcae71b917.\n\nThe shared TypedProgram now carries an optional Arc\u003cCheckerInterface\u003e: interned source export names, native function identities, generic signatures, parameter ownership, aliases, referenced property views/readonly facts and array return minimum/parameter provenance. Views have module-qualified identities; property names stay source names. Signatures and views share immutable Arc data. Parameter ownership lives beside each parameter type in one vector; small source field lists use SmallVec. Ordinary scripts do not construct an export interface. No executable bodies are copied to check an importer, and only views referenced by exported types/signatures enter the interface.\n\nThe existing CompiledImport and Snapshot paths serialize this metadata. Compiled import schema is now 5 and snapshot schema 11; older artifacts must be rebuilt. HIR/bytecode layout is unchanged. Type IDs from supplied dependencies are reserved before the importer allocates types. Other frontend/program constructors use None; selective CompiledImport copies retain the interface by Arc.\n\nparse_module_with_dependencies consumes host-resolved ModuleDependency programs, including Snapshot::module_interface programs that contain no function declarations. Supported syntax in this slice: exported functions/interfaces/type aliases, local export aliases, named value/type imports with aliases, explicit type-only imports, and bare side-effect imports. Repeated named requests within one source module combine before the shared import chain, including a bare import without losing named signature selection. Unused named imports and import {} are elided to match the pinned TypeScript default. Bare imports can evaluate script dependencies without exported symbols. Type-only/elided imports do not request runtime code. The separate TypeScript type/value namespaces are preserved when one export name names both a type and a function.\n\nThe generic snapshot regression now parses real imports against a compact dependency interface; it no longer concatenates library source, removes declarations or reuses the library's native namespace for the caller. Existing generic bodies remain deferred and specialize through the shared compiler.\n\nValidation:\n- 60/60 TypeScript Rust tests in default and LLVM/allocation builds. The final interface/snapshot tests and Clippy also pass after the dual-namespace import regression was added.\n- Cross-module source and snapshot execution passes before promotion, through Cranelift/LLVM and mixed tiers. Native float GEPs remain in caller HIR. Static release inserts exactly one free for the object, array header and array buffer despite generic and array aliases.\n- Private export/name diagnostics, readonly errors, checked-mode weakening rejection, type/value namespaces, artifact and compact snapshot metadata, type-only elision and bare-import initializer evaluation are covered. A trap in a snapshot initializer distinguishes type checking from evaluation.\n- The unused snapshot template allocation regression passes separately; unused bodies stay encoded and only the reached templates decode within the existing budget.\n- 657/657 conformance runs: 219 fixtures across interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM profiles. Four export fixtures pass the live TypeScript 6.0.2/Node oracle in all profiles (12 live comparisons). Independent two-file Node oracle checks accept the exact integration sources and produce the same -3, 5, 11.5 results; the dual-namespace example also agrees.\n- 90 shared embed tests pass (83 library tests, six module-initializer tests, one borrowed-snapshot test). 57 targeted compiler tests pass (43 lowering/pipeline/prelowered tests and 14 effect tests).\n- Compiler test targets compile, as do Python/Lua 